WEBVTT
TITLE Error Detection and Recovery in Parsers: the Blank Cell, Panic Mode, Phrase-Level Repair and Error Productions — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
An LR parser looks up one cell: the state on top of the stack, and the token on the input.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A blank cell is the error. The GOTO table is never asked about a token, so it cannot signal one.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
On id + ) id the SLR parser stops at the third token, the ")", in state 7, with stack 0 3 7.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Three reductions came first — F -> id, T -> F, E -> T — and none of them was wrong.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A prefix is viable when some valid sentence of the grammar begins with exactly that string.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Every configuration of a correct parse has a viable prefix on its stack: 48 checked, 48 viable.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
At an error the stack is still viable, and the stack plus the token is not.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Across 777 erroneous strings, all 777 times the stack was viable and stack plus token was not.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A reduction is not an error signal. The parser reduces only when its table says so.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
SLR writes reduces into every column of FOLLOW, which is grammar-wide, so it reduces on lookaheads it has not earned.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Before giving up on the 777 erroneous strings, SLR took 344 reductions and canonical LR(1) took 218.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
On ( id, SLR reduces three times before the error; canonical LR(1) reduces none.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Panic mode: pop states until one has a GOTO on some nonterminal A, then discard tokens until one can follow A.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
On id id the parser pops one state, discards one token, pushes GOTO(0, E) = 3 and carries on to acceptance.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
On ( ) it pops nothing and discards nothing: GOTO(1, E) = 6 lets the empty parentheses stand for a missing expression.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Two numbers describe every recovery: states popped, and tokens discarded.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
On id + ) id the rule as written does not terminate: it pushes T at state 10, reduces to E, and errors again.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The stack and the token come back to where they were, so the same recovery is chosen forever.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The guard: if the same stack and position recur, delete the token. One forced deletion breaks the cycle.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The parser then accepts — but it accepts id + with the ")" and the operand deleted, not the program you wrote.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
In an LL parser the synchronising set for a nonterminal A is FOLLOW(A).

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
If the token is in FOLLOW(A), pop A and continue. If it is not, discard the token and try again.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
On b b c: one error, one token discarded, and the parse finishes. On b b b: three errors, two discards, one pop.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
FOLLOW is a property of the grammar, so the set can accept a token that cannot occur at this point.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Phrase-level recovery changes the input instead of skipping it: insert, delete or replace one token.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
For five terminals that is ten candidates at the error point: one deletion, five insertions, four replacements.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
On id + ) id exactly one of the ten repairs parses to the end: delete the ")".

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The repair that reaches furthest is replace ")" by "(", which still fails — so reach and acceptance are different tests.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
An error production puts the mistake in the grammar: F -> ( error ) and E -> error.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
That costs three states — 12 to 15 — and one new conflict: state 7 must choose between shifting ")" and reducing E -> error.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Recovery with the error token: pop until error can be shifted, shift it, then delete offending tokens while in error mode.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
On id + ) id that is one report, two pops and two silent deletions — and an error at the end of input cannot be repaired this way.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Four strategies on one string: no recovery, panic mode, phrase-level repair, error productions.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
No recovery reports once and stops; panic mode reports four times and accepts a repaired string.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Phrase-level tries ten repairs and finds one; error productions report once and delete two tokens without a message.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Every strategy accepts something. Only one of them accepts the program you wrote, and that is none of them.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Five steps: find the blank cell, name the token, choose the recovery, guard against looping, and report.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
The four traps: calling a reduction an error, trusting panic mode to terminate, reading recovery as acceptance, and forgetting end of input.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
The exam asks which action happens, in what order, and how many tokens move.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Keep the two counts — states popped, tokens discarded — and the rest follows.

